The "already in right place" check: insertBefore is idempotent when the computed position equals current. If before && tgt.previousElementSibling === drag.li → already directly before tgt → skip. If !before && tgt.nextElementSibling? tgt.nextSibling === drag.li → already directly after → skip. Add these guards to avoid needless flip animations each move:

```
const next = before ? tgt : tgt.nextSibling;
if (next === drag.li) return; // уже на месте (прямо перед next)
flip(...)
```
insertBefore(li, next): if li immediately before next, no-op. So guard `if (next === drag.li) return;` — but drag.li could be next? No: next is either tgt (a sibling ≠ drag.li since we skip drag.li in scan... wait tgt could equal drag.li in the !tgt branch if list has only the dragged item — impossible, 4 items). next===drag.li covers "already before tgt". For "already after tgt": next = tgt.nextSibling === drag.li → insertBefore(drag.li, drag.li) no-op → harmless (flip would measure & no-op, but flip triggers rAF churn). insertBefore(drag.li, drag.li) actually no-ops in DOM. flip would still run animation reset — minor. Add combined guard: `if (next === drag.li) return;` covers both? "after tgt" case: next = tgt.nextSibling; if that's drag.li → insertBefore(li, li) = no-op → return. Yes covered.

setOver(drag.li)? If tgt computed equals drag.li (can't per scan skip, but !tgt branch guards). Fine.

Also keep is-over semantics: highlight target under pointer. OK.

One more edge: pointercancel on scroll (touch scrolling) — during drag with touch-action none on handle, scroll won't start from handle. If browser cancels, onPointerUp cleans up. Good.

